---
/*
 * Assets go in:
 *   public/images/case-studies/data-collection-system/
 *   public/videos/case-studies/data-collection-system/
 * Screenshots referenced below aren't in yet — each spot is marked with a
 * TODO comment describing what goes there.
 */

import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CaseStudyHero from '../../components/case-study/CaseStudyHero.astro';
import CaseStudyToc from '../../components/case-study/CaseStudyToc.astro';
import SectionDivider from '../../components/case-study/SectionDivider.astro';
import FeatureCard from '../../components/case-study/FeatureCard.astro';
import InsightCard from '../../components/case-study/InsightCard.astro';
import BackButton from '../../components/case-study/BackButton.astro';
import '../../styles/case-study.css';

const tocItems = [
	{ href: '#the-problem', label: 'The Problem' },
	{ href: '#the-solution', label: 'The Solution' },
	{ href: '#process', label: 'Process' },
	{ href: '#outcomes', label: 'Outcomes' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Data Collection System"
	description="Designing a streamlined data collection process for internal and external users."
>
	<article class="cs-page">
		<div class="cs-back-button-row">
			<BackButton />
		</div>

		<CaseStudyHero
			title="Designing a Streamlined Data Collection Process"
			summary="I designed a system that replaced two disconnected survey tools with a single, self-service way for Fable's testers to answer dynamic profile questions, freeing the internal team from manually reconciling data and generating surveys by hand."
			meta={[
				{ label: 'Role', value: 'Junior UX Designer' },
				{ label: 'Timeline', value: '1 month' },
				{ label: 'Project Type', value: 'UX Design' },
				{ label: 'Team Type', value: '2 developers, 1 product manager, 1 designer (me)' },
				{ label: 'Year', value: '2026' },
			]}
		>
			{/* TODO: add hero image or video */}
		</CaseStudyHero>

		<CaseStudyToc items={tocItems} />

		<SectionDivider id="the-problem" title="The Problem" />

		<section class="cs-two-col">
			<div class="cs-two-col-text">
				<h3>Fable's Panel of Testers</h3>
				<p>
					Fable's internal team recruits, trains, and manages a panel of testers (people with disabilities who use
					different assistive technologies) so Fable's clients can test the accessibility of their websites and apps
					with real users.
				</p>
				<p>
					The Fable platform already tracked static information about the testers.
					But clients sometimes depended on more specific, changeable data, for example, whether a tester uses a
					particular app or has a particular skill.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="cs-two-col-media">
				<img
					class="cs-rounded"
					src="/images/case-studies/data-collection-system/AT.avif"
					alt="A Freedom Scientific refreshable braille display with blue buttons and a row of braille cells, connected to an open laptop on a desk"
				/>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="cs-highlight">
			<h3>No Central System for Dynamic Data</h3>
			<p>
				These dynamic questions changed often, needed regular updates, and varied in priority — unlike the fixed,
				hardcoded fields (like assistive technology type) the platform was built around. As a result, these data was
				collected two ways: through ongoing surveys inside the Fable platform, and through a separate external
				survey tool. There was no single place where it all lived.
			</p>
			{/* TODO: add screenshot — flowchart of the current, fragmented data collection process */}
		</section>

		<section class="cs-stack">
			<h3>That split created friction on both sides</h3>
			<div class="cs-row friction-row">
				<div class="friction-col">
					<p class="cs-bold">For the internal team</p>
					<ul class="cs-rule-list">
						<li><strong class="highlight">Data lived in different places</strong>, tracked manually across two survey tools</li>
						<li><strong class="highlight">Survey answers weren't connected to the tester's profile</strong>, so the team had to view static and dynamic data separately</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="friction-col">
					<p class="cs-bold">For testers</p>
					<ul class="cs-rule-list">
						<li>They sometimes had to re-answer questions they'd already answered, <strong class="highlight">with no explanation why</strong></li>
						<li><strong class="highlight">They had to navigate multiple places to answer questions</strong>, which was especially confusing during onboarding</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<SectionDivider id="the-solution" title="The Solution" />

		<p class="cs-section-intro solution-intro">
			The end result was a system that let the internal team program questions for testers,
			replacing the two-tool survey process <strong class="highlight">with one connected experience.</strong>
		</p>

		<section class="cs-stack solution-cards">
			<FeatureCard
				title="Question Builder"
				subtitle="The internal team can now build custom questions directly in the Fable platform, which are automatically sent to testers."
			>
				<ul>
					<li>Supports multiple choice, single choice, open text, and branching questions</li>
					<li>Questions can have an expiration date, prompting testers to give an updated response</li>
					<li>The team can set question priority — for example, surfacing certain questions during onboarding when recruiting for a specific need</li>
				</ul>
				<Fragment slot="image">{/* TODO: add screenshot — question builder interface */}</Fragment>
			</FeatureCard>

			<FeatureCard title="A Questions Section on the Tester Profile" subtitle="Testers get a dedicated space to view every question and their answers.">
				<ul>
					<li>A single page shows which questions are answered and which need a new or updated response</li>
					<li>Unanswered or outdated questions surface in two places — the tester's profile and their homepage — with a button to answer everything in one survey-style flow</li>
					<li>When a response needs updating, the profile acknowledges the tester's previous answer rather than just re-asking</li>
					<li>A progress chart shows how many questions are answered, need updating, or are still unanswered</li>
					<li>Clear status messaging throughout: answered, needs an update, or not yet answered</li>
				</ul>
				<Fragment slot="image">{/* TODO: add screenshot — tester's question dashboard */}</Fragment>
			</FeatureCard>

			<FeatureCard
				title="Data Management System"
				subtitle="The internal team gets a table overview of all tester responses to dynamic questions, with an at-a-glance count of how many testers answered each one — plus the option to drill into an individual tester's full profile for an in-depth view."
			>
				<Fragment slot="image">{/* TODO: add screenshot — data management table */}</Fragment>
			</FeatureCard>
		</section>

		<SectionDivider id="process" title="Process" />

		<section class="cs-two-col">
			<div class="cs-two-col-text">
				<h3>Understanding the Problem</h3>
				<p>
					I started with interviews with Fable's internal team, then mapped a user flowchart to pinpoint where the
					friction was happening:
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Questions were being asked at different points in the tester journey (some during onboarding, some after), which led to testers re-answering the same question so the data could live in the Fable platform</li>
					<li>Surveys were sent either in-platform or via a link to an external tool, so both the internal team and testers had to work across two platforms</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="cs-two-col-media">
				{/* TODO: add screenshot — user flowchart from internal team interviews */}
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="cs-stack">
			<h3>Competitive Research</h3>
			<p>
				I looked at how other platforms encouraged users to complete their own data. Most used benefit-driven
				language, e.g., &ldquo;Complete your profile to be eligible for more opportunities.&rdquo;
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="cs-stack">
			<h3>Work Distribution</h3>
			<p>
				Working closely with the developers and product manager, we split the work by timeline: developers led the
				question builder (with my input), and I focused on the tester-facing experience.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="cs-stack">
			<h3>User Interviews with Testers</h3>
			<p>I interviewed testers to understand their current experience answering these questions.</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Based on the competitive research, I initially assumed framing answers around eligibility (&ldquo;this increases your chances&rdquo;) would work well</li>
				<li>But testers told me that framing added pressure — especially when their honest answer was &ldquo;no.&rdquo; I had to balance motivating language with not making testers feel penalized for their answers</li>
				<li>I also learned that testers generally liked filling out surveys, which meant I could lean on our existing survey pattern rather than reinvent it</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="cs-two-col">
			<div class="cs-two-col-text">
				<h3>Iteration 1</h3>
				<p>My first design included:</p>
				<ul>
					<li>A list of questions testers could answer, edit, and review</li>
					<li>Text at the top flagging any unanswered questions</li>
					<li>A call-to-action button that opened all unanswered questions in a survey format</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="cs-two-col-media">
				{/* TODO: add screenshot — Iteration 1 wireframe */}
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="cs-stack cs-on-light">
			<h3>Feedback & Iteration 2</h3>
			<p>Two issues came out of feedback on Iteration 1:</p>
			{/* TODO: add screenshot — Iteration 2 with progress chart */}
			<InsightCard title="Questions needed branching.">
				For example, a &ldquo;no&rdquo; answer might need no follow-up, while a &ldquo;yes&rdquo; might need
				clarifying questions. In Iteration 1, each question lived on its own card — I redesigned each card to work
				as either a standalone question or a self-contained mini-survey, so branching stayed grouped with its
				parent question.
			</InsightCard>
			<InsightCard title="Finishing didn't feel satisfying.">
				The unanswered-question count at the top of the page simply disappeared once everything was answered, with
				no sense of completion. I replaced it with a progress chart showing how many questions were answered,
				needed updating, or were still unanswered. I also added the date a tester last answered an expiring
				question, after feedback that testers wanted that context when asked to update a response.
			</InsightCard>
		</section>

		<SectionDivider id="outcomes" title="Outcomes" />

		<section class="cs-stack cs-stack--last">
			<p class="cs-h4-size">
				The new system removed two of the biggest manual burdens from the internal team's workflow:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>They no longer needed to manually cross-reference two separate survey tools to see a tester's full picture — static and dynamic data now live together in one profile</li>
				<li>They no longer needed to manually build and send out surveys to keep tester data current — the question builder handles this automatically, with expiration dates prompting testers for updates on its own</li>
			</ul>

			<BackButton />
		</section>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	/* The two lists can have a different number of bullets, so stretch both
	   columns to the row's full height and let the list grow into it —
	   otherwise cs-row's vertical centering leaves their hairlines
	   misaligned. */
	.friction-row {
		align-items: stretch;
	}

	.friction-col {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--space-3);
	}

	.friction-col .cs-rule-list {
		flex: 1;
	}

	/* 32px isn't on the 4/8/16/24/40/64 spacing scale — a one-off gap above
	   this intro line specifically. */
	.solution-intro {
		margin-top: 2rem;
		padding-bottom: 0;
	}

	.solution-cards {
		padding-top: 2rem;
	}

	.highlight {
		color: var(--color-text-on-light);
		background: var(--color-accent-yellow);
	}
</style>
